                    { "label": "2",  "cells": [{ "kana": "にふん", "en": "2 minutes" }, { "term": "ふん", "en": "plain" }] },
                    { "label": "3",  "cells": [{ "kana": "さんぷん", "en": "3 minutes" }, { "term": "ぷん", "en": "after n" }] },
                    { "label": "4",  "cells": [{ "kana": "よんぷん", "en": "4 minutes" }, { "term": "ぷん", "en": "after n" }] },
                    { "label": "5",  "cells": [{ "kana": "ごふん", "en": "5 minutes" }, { "term": "ふん", "en": "plain" }] },
                    { "label": "6",  "cells": [{ "kana": "ろっぷん", "en": "6 minutes" }, { "term": "ぷん", "en": "small tsu" }] },
                    { "label": "7",  "cells": [{ "kana": "ななふん", "en": "7 minutes" }, { "term": "ふん", "en": "plain" }] },
                    { "label": "8",  "cells": [{ "kana": "はっぷん", "en": "8 minutes" }, { "term": "ぷん", "en": "small tsu" }] },
                    { "label": "9",  "cells": [{ "kana": "きゅうふん", "en": "9 minutes" }, { "term": "ふん", "en": "plain" }] },
                    { "label": "10", "cells": [{ "kana": "じゅっぷん", "en": "10 minutes" }, { "term": "ぷん", "en": "small tsu" }] },
                    { "label": "15", "cells": [{ "kana": "じゅうごふん", "en": "15 minutes" }, { "term": "ふん", "en": "ends in 5" }] },
                    { "label": "30", "cells": [{ "kana": "さんじゅっぷん", "en": "30 minutes" }, { "term": "はん", "en": "or just han" }] },
                    { "label": "?",  "cells": [{ "kana": "なんぷん", "en": "how many minutes?" }, { "term": "ぷん", "en": "after n" }] }
                ]
            },
            {
                "title": "putting it together",
                "columns": ["said out loud"],
                "rows": [
                    { "label": "a.m.",   "cells": [{ "kana": "ごぜんくじ", "romaji": "gozen kuji", "en": "9 a.m. — gozen goes first, unlike English" }] },
                    { "label": "p.m.",   "cells": [{ "kana": "ごごさんじ", "romaji": "gogo sanji", "en": "3 p.m." }] },
                    { "label": "half",   "cells": [{ "kana": "さんじはん", "romaji": "sanji han", "en": "half past three" }] },
                    { "label": ":15",    "cells": [{ "kana": "しちじじゅうごふん", "romaji": "shichiji juugofun", "en": "7:15" }] },
                    { "label": ":45",    "cells": [{ "kana": "よじよんじゅうごふん", "romaji": "yoji yonjuugofun", "en": "4:45" }] },
                    { "label": "to",     "cells": [{ "kana": "はちじごふんまえ", "romaji": "hachiji gofun mae", "en": "five to eight — mae means before" }] },
                    { "label": "span",   "cells": [{ "kana": "くじからごじまで", "romaji": "kuji kara goji made", "en": "from nine to five" }] },
                    { "label": "asking", "cells": [{ "kana": "いまなんじですか", "romaji": "ima nanji desu ka", "en": "what time is it now?" }] }
                ]
            }
        ],
        "rules": [
            {
                "title": "Hour then minute, nothing in between",
                "body": "sanji juugofun is 3:15. Japanese has no word doing the work of 'past' or 'to' — you say the hour, then the minutes, and stop."
            },
            {
                "title": "Three hours lie about their number",
                "body": "4 is yoji, not yonji. 7 is shichiji, not nanaji. 9 is kuji, not kyuuji. The other nine hours are just the number plus ji, so these three are the whole memorisation cost."
            },
            {
                "title": "pun after a stopped sound",
                "body": "1, 3, 4, 6, 8 and 10 end in an n or a small tsu, and the f hardens to p: ippun, sanpun, yonpun, roppun, happun, juppun. 2, 5, 7 and 9 stay soft: nifun, gofun, nanafun, kyuufun. Same rule for compounds — 15 is juugofun, 30 is sanjuppun."
            },
            {
                "title": "4, 7 and 9 switch sides",
                "body": "The traps invert between the columns: yoji but yonpun, shichiji but nanafun, kuji but kyuufun. If you learn one column and guess the other, these three are where you'll be wrong."
            },
            {
                "title": "han saves you a mouthful",
                "body": "sanji han — half past three. Nobody counts out sanji sanjuppun when talking. And gozen / gogo go in front of the hour, not after it: gozen kuji, gogo sanji."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "id": "going",
        "title": "Going somewhere",
        "blurb": "One shape carries most travel sentences: when + how + where + verb. The slots are easy; the particles are what goes wrong, so this sheet is mostly them.",
        "legend": [
            { "term": "de", "gloss": "by / using" },
            { "term": "ni", "gloss": "to / at" },
            { "term": "e",  "gloss": "toward — written he" },
            { "term": "to", "gloss": "together with" }
        ],
        "grids": [
            {
                "title": "the sentence, slot by slot",
                "columns": ["example", "particle", "watch out"],
                "rows": [
                    { "label": "when", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "せんしゅう", "en": "last week" },
                        { "term": "—", "en": "relative time takes none" },
                        { "term": "ni", "en": "only with a number: kuji ni" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "how", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "でんしゃで", "romaji": "densha de", "en": "by train" },
                        { "term": "de", "en": "the means" },
                        { "term": "aruite", "en": "on foot takes no de" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "who with", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "ともだちと", "romaji": "tomodachi to", "en": "with a friend" },
                        { "term": "to", "en": "a companion" },
                        { "term": "hitori de", "en": "alone uses de, not to" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "where", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "びょういんに", "romaji": "byouin ni", "en": "to the hospital" },
                        { "term": "ni / e", "en": "the destination" },
                        { "term": "e", "en": "written he, said e" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "verb", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "いきます", "en": "go" },
                        { "term": "—", "en": "kimasu come, kaerimasu go home" },
                        { "term": "last", "en": "the verb always ends the sentence" }
                    ] }
                ]
            },
            {
                "title": "the ones that trip you up",
                "columns": ["phrase", "why it is like that"],
                "rows": [
                    { "label": "by train", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "でんしゃで", "romaji": "densha de", "en": "by train" },
                        { "term": "de", "en": "every vehicle: basu de, jitensha de, hikouki de, fune de" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "on foot", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "あるいて", "en": "on foot", "odd": true },
                        { "term": "—", "en": "already a te-form verb, so no de — aruite ikimasu" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "alone", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "ひとりで", "romaji": "hitori de", "en": "alone" },
                        { "term": "de", "en": "a state you are in, so it keeps de" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "with a friend", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "ともだちと", "romaji": "tomodachi to", "en": "with a friend", "odd": true },
                        { "term": "to", "en": "a companion never takes de" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "to the station", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "えきに", "romaji": "eki ni", "en": "to the station" },
                        { "term": "ni / e", "en": "e is written he — same trick as konnichiwa" }
                    ] }
                ]
            },
            {
                "title": "the four endings",
                "columns": ["said out loud", "form"],
                "rows": [
                    { "label": "now", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "いきます", "en": "go / will go" },
                        { "term": "-masu", "en": "present or future, both" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "past", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "いきました", "en": "went" },
                        { "term": "-mashita", "en": "" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "not", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "いきません", "en": "do not go" },
                        { "term": "-masen", "en": "" }
                    ] },
                    { "label": "not, past", "cells": [
                        { "kana": "いきませんでした", "en": "did not go" },
                        { "term": "-masen deshita", "en": "two words — the past sits on deshita" }
                    ] }
                ]
            }
        ],
        "rules": [
            {
                "title": "The shape",
                "body": "when + how + where + verb, and the topic is usually dropped: senshuu, densha de toukyou e ikimashita. watashi wa only goes in when you are switching who the sentence is about."
            },
            {
                "title": "e is spelled he",
                "body": "The destination particle is written he and said e — the same trick as konnichiwa. ni does the same job and is the commoner pick: byouin ni ikimasu. e leans on the direction, ni on the arrival."
            },
            {
                "title": "de is the means, to is the company",
                "body": "Anything you travel by takes de: densha de, jitensha de, takushii de. A person you go with takes to: tomodachi to ikimashita. hitori de looks like an exception and is not — alone is a state, not a companion."
            },
            {
                "title": "aruite takes nothing",
                "body": "aruite ikimasu — I go on foot. It is already a te-form verb, so nothing follows it. aruite de is always wrong."
            },
            {
                "title": "Time words: ni or nothing",
                "body": "kyou, ashita, senshuu, raigetsu, mainichi attach bare. Time with a number or a calendar slot takes ni: kuji ni, shichigatsu ni, tanjoubi ni. Weekdays go either way — getsuyoubi ni ikimasu or getsuyoubi, ikimasu."
            }
        ]
    },
