        "rules": [
            {
                "title": "Check the last kana, then check underneath it",
                "body": "An adjective that ends in い written in hiragana is an i-adjective. kirei and yuumei end in the same sound, but there the い is part of the kanji reading — 綺麗, 有名 — so they take na. There are only a handful of those; learn them as exceptions rather than looking for a rule."
            },
            {
                "title": "i-adjectives conjugate, na-adjectives don't",
                "body": "The い ending carries tense and negation itself. A na-adjective never changes shape: desu, deshita and ja nai desu do all the work, exactly as they would after a noun. That is the real split between the two families — everything else follows from it."
            },
            {
                "title": "na only appears in front of a noun",
                "body": "shizuka desu has no na. shizuka na heya does. The family is named after that one position, which is also the only place you have to remember it."
            },
            {
                "title": "The negative is itself an i-adjective",
                "body": "nai ends in い and conjugates like any other i-adjective. That is why ookiku nai becomes ookiku nakatta desu — and why shizuka ja nai becomes shizuka ja nakatta desu. Both families end up running the same negative, which is the reason ja nai desu is worth preferring: learn nai once and every negative in this sheet falls out of it."
            },
            {
                "title": "ii is the exception worth drilling",
                "body": "ii desu, but yoku nai desu, yokatta desu, yoku nakatta desu. Every form except the plain present is built from yoi, and ii is common enough that the wrong form is noticeable."
            },
            {
                "title": "Your textbook will say ja arimasen",
                "body": "shizuka ja arimasen and shizuka ja arimasen deshita mean exactly the same as the forms above and are what Minna no Nihongo drills, so recognise them. ja nai desu is the everyday spoken version and keeps the negative identical to the i-adjectives, which is one pattern to hold instead of two. de wa arimasen is the same thing again, formal and mostly written — ja is just the contraction of de wa."
            }
        ]
